=== §1  ball3Drive (KF:449): the generated group is SO(3), transitive on the sphere
ok    1.01 cyc3 matrix has det +1 (cyc3 ∈ SO(3))
ok    1.02 cyc3 e_z = e_x
ok    1.03 cyc3 Rz(t) cyc3^-1 = Rx(t) for symbolic t (conjugate flow = rotation about e_x)
ok    1.04 rot3 group law Rz(s)Rz(t) = Rz(s+t) (KF:366 restated)
ok    1.05 N = Rz(pi) is involutive and moves e_x
ok    1.06 D9 witness: cyc3 Rz(pi) cyc3^-1 e_z = -e_z while Rz(s) e_z = e_z for every s
ok    1.07 Euler word: Rz(phi+pi/2) Rx(theta) e_z = (sinθcosφ, sinθsinφ, cosθ) for symbolic θ, φ
ok    1.08 Rx(theta) = cyc3 Rz(theta) cyc3^-1, so the word lies in <rot3(R), cyc3>
ok    1.09 stabilizer of e_z in SO(3) is {Rz}: Rz(γ) fixes e_z (symbolic)
ok    1.10.0 exact zxz Euler word in flow and cyc3-conjugate flow reproduces Cayley rotation from skew(1,2,3)
ok    1.10.1 exact zxz Euler word in flow and cyc3-conjugate flow reproduces Cayley rotation from skew(1/2,-3,5)
ok    1.10.2 exact zxz Euler word in flow and cyc3-conjugate flow reproduces Cayley rotation from skew(-2,0,1)
ok    1.10.3 rational rotation [[-24/25, -7/25, 0], [7/25, -24/25, 0], [0, 0, 1]] is a flow member Rz
ok    1.10.4 exact zxz Euler word in flow and cyc3-conjugate flow reproduces Cayley rotation from skew(3,-1,2/3)
ok    1.10.5 exact zxz Euler word in flow and cyc3-conjugate flow reproduces Cayley rotation from skew(1,1,0)
ok    1.11 Lie algebra generated by Lz and cyc3 Lz cyc3^-1 has dimension 3 = dim so(3)
ok    1.12 mutation J = Rz(pi/2): J Rz(t) J^-1 = Rz(t) — D9 fails, Lie dim 1, orbit of e_z is {e_z}
ok    1.13 mutation J = diag(1,-1,-1): J Rz(t) J^-1 = Rz(-t) — D9 fails, J e_z = -e_z, Lie dim 1
NOTE  1.N1 G = <rot3(R), cyc3> ⊇ {Rz(α)Rx(β)Rz(γ)} = SO(3) as an abstract group (1.03, 1.10, zxz Euler); cyc3 ∈ SO(3) (1.01), so G = SO(3) exactly. No closure is taken; D3 is not used because rot3 is surjective onto the circle by construction. Boundary states of ball3 = unit sphere (⇒: proof of supportingEffectComplete_ball3 KF:1056–1062; ⇐: y = −x). G is transitive on it (1.07).

=== §2  every drive on ball3: transitive given D3; D3 load-bearing
ok    2.01.0 rational J (Cayley 1,2,3): J e_z ∉ {±e_z} and Lie<Lz, J Lz J^-1> has dim 3
ok    2.01.1 rational J (Cayley 1/3,0,0): J e_z ∉ {±e_z} and Lie<Lz, J Lz J^-1> has dim 3
ok    2.01.2 rational J (Cayley 0,1/5,1): J e_z ∉ {±e_z} and Lie<Lz, J Lz J^-1> has dim 3
ok    2.02 improper J (reflection ⟂ (1,0,1)) preserves ball3, J e_z = -e_x ∉ {±e_z}, Lie dim 3
NOTE  2.N1 Written: an affine bijection f of R^3 with f(ball3) ⊆ ball3 and f^-1(ball3) ⊆ ball3 maps the ball onto itself, fixes its unique centre of symmetry 0, and its linear part A and A^-1 are norm-contractions, so A ∈ O(3). D2+D3+D6: t ↦ flow t is a nontrivial continuous homomorphism R → SO(3) (flow t = (flow t/2)^2 has det +1), image = full circle {R_a(ωt)}. D9 with D7/D8: J ∈ O(3); if J a = ±a then J R_a(θ) J^-1 = R_a(±θ) is a flow member, so D9 forces J a ∉ {±a}.
NOTE  2.N2 Written (cap argument, no Lie theory): G ⊇ circles about a and b = Ja, γ = ∠(a,b) ∈ (0, π/2] after b ↦ −b. As β runs over [0, π], ∠(a, R_b(β)a) runs continuously over [0, 2γ]; R_a(α) is transitive on each circle of fixed angle about a; so G·a ⊇ cap(a, 2γ). G acts by isometries, so g·cap(a,2γ) = cap(ga,2γ): G·a is a union of closed caps of radius 2γ about its own points, hence open, and closed (a limit v of points u ∈ G·a lies in cap(u,2γ) for u close to v). S² connected ⇒ G·a = S². The stabilizer of a in SO(3) is R_a(R) ⊆ G, so G ⊇ SO(3). Citation-level alternative: Lowenthal, Rocky Mountain J. Math. 1 (1971) 575–586 (finite generation of SO(3) by two circle subgroups).
NOTE  2.N3 D3 is load-bearing (countermodel, written, uses a Hamel basis): choose an additive φ: R → R with φ(1) = π and φ(R) = Qπ (Hamel basis B ∋ 1, φ(1) = π, φ(b) = 0 for b ≠ 1). flow t := Rz(φ(t)), t0 := 1, J := cyc3. D1, D2 (φ additive), D4, D5 (Rz(π)^2 = 1), D6, D7, D8 and D9 (witness of 1.06 at t = 1) hold; only D3 fails. The generated group is countable, so the orbit of e_z is countable and not S^2.

=== §3  abstract drivable bodies: transitivity that does not follow
ok    3.01 bidisk drive: F(t) = R(t)⊕I preserves (|x|²,|y|²); J = block swap exchanges them
ok    3.02 bidisk: F group law, N = F(pi) involutive, moves (e1,0)
ok    3.03 bidisk D9: J F(pi) J^-1 (0,e1) = (0,-e1) while F(s)(0,e1) = (0,e1) for all s
ok    3.04 (e1,e1) and (e1,0) are boundary states (KF:130 witness y = −x: first block norm² (1+2ε)² > 1 ∀ε>0)
ok    3.05 G_D-invariant unordered pair {|x|²,|y|²}: {1,1} at (e1,e1) vs {1,0} at (e1,0) — not one G_D-orbit
ok    3.06 midpoint identity (e1,0) = ((e1,e1)+(e1,-e1))/2, so (e1,0) is not extreme [(e1,e1) is extreme: written, product of extreme points]; affine automorphisms preserve extremality
NOTE  3.N1 Bidisk: drivable (3.01–3.03; D3 by continuity of cos, sin), compact, convex, boundary states (e1,e1), (e1,0) in different orbits of Aut(Ω) ⊇ G_D. G_D ⊇ SO(2)×SO(2) is transitive on ext Ω = S¹×S¹ but not on the boundary.
ok    3.07 cone over B³: Rz(t)⊕1 and cyc3⊕1 preserve |x|² and h (so preserve Ω = {0≤h≤1, |x|≤1−h})
ok    3.08 cone: D9 witness transported (cyc3⊕1)(Rz(pi)⊕1)(cyc3⊕1)^-1 maps (0,0,1,0) to (0,0,-1,0)
ok    3.09 cone: every generator fixes the apex, so the G_D-orbit of the apex is {apex} while (e1,0) is moved
ok    3.10 cone: the apex is isolated in ext Ω = S²×{0} ∪ {apex} (squared distance to S²×{0} is exactly 2)
NOTE  3.N2 Cone: affine automorphisms are homeomorphisms of Ω preserving ext Ω, hence its isolated points; the apex is the only isolated extreme point, so Aut(Ω) fixes it. Drivable compact convex body on which even Aut(Ω) is not transitive on extreme points.
ok    3.11 B⁴ with flow Rz(t)⊕1, J = cyc3⊕1: x4 is G_D-invariant, so e4 and e1 (both on S³) are in different G_D-orbits
NOTE  3.N3 B⁴: Ω is a ball and drivable, but G_D (≅ SO(3)) is not transitive on its boundary S³. Drivability does not give boundary transitivity of the drive group even when the body is a ball (dim ≥ 4).
ok    3.12 planar control: a reflection conjugates R(t) to R(-t) (given c²+s²=1); a rotation commutes with R(t)
NOTE  3.N4 What does follow from the fields alone (written): (i) G_D|Ω is non-abelian (D9 says J flow(t) J^-1 ≠ flow(t) on Ω); (ii) for compact Ω in finite dimension, the flow moves some extreme point (an affine map fixing ext Ω fixes Ω), and by D3 its flow orbit is a non-constant continuous curve in ext Ω, so ext Ω is uncountable; (iii) the closure of G_D|Ω is a compact group, orthogonal for an averaged inner product about the centroid. Nothing more about orbits follows: 3.05 (boundary), 3.09 (extreme points), 3.11 (ball, dim 4).

=== §4  sourcing: what the landed matrix objects generate on the qubit Bloch ball (imported C)
ok    4.01 diagonal phase diag(e^{ia1}, e^{ia2}) acts on the Bloch ball as Rz(a2 - a1): fixes e_z
ok    4.02 permMatrix swap acts as diag(1,-1,-1) = Rx(pi): sends e_z to -e_z
ok    4.03 every monomial unitary perm·diag on Fin 2 sends e_z to ±e_z (both families, symbolic phases)
NOTE  4.N1 Hence the monomial (substratum, SC:180) unitary group of Fin 2 acts on S² through O(2)_z = rotations preserving the line Re_z: the orbit of a pole is {±e_z}; not transitive. A continuous flow inside it is diagonal (identity component), i.e. Rz; a monomial J then sends e_z to ±e_z, so D9 fails. Group-level form of substratum_residual (SC:383) on the Bloch ball; matches Thread G key result 5.
ok    4.04 phaseGate 0 = diag(i,1) acts as Rz(-pi/2) (a monomial, supplied by phaseOperator_supplied SC:333)
ok    4.05 gateFlow(swap, t) acts on the Bloch ball as Rx(pi·t) for symbolic t
ok    4.06 gateFlow(swap, 1) = permMat swap (LA:84 restated) and gateFlow(1/2) has two nonzero entries per column
ok    4.07 e^{-itX} = e^{-it}·gateFlow(swap, 2t/pi): transition flow = gate flow up to a global (monomial) phase
ok    4.08 with J = phaseGate 0: J·Rx(pi t)·J^-1 = Ry(-pi t) — the off-axis clause D9 holds (axis e_x ↦ -e_y)
ok    4.09 Bloch drive (flow Rx(pi t), t0 = 1 so N = Rx(pi) = image of the swap, J = image of phaseGate 0): N involutive and moves e_z; Lie<Lx, J Lx J^-1> has dim 3
NOTE  4.N2 So ⟨gateFlow(swap, R), monomial unitaries of Fin 2⟩ acts on the Bloch sphere as SO(3) (Rx(R) and its J-conjugate Ry(R) generate SO(3) by §1's Euler argument with the axes relabelled) and is transitive on it. The only generator outside the substratum class is the gate flow at non-integer times (4.06; LA:138). Matrix-level kernel counterpart: exactReachability_of_hcontrol (OR:625) applied to transition_hermitian (LRS:211), ctrl_unitary (LRS:293), hControl_star (LRS:352) gives ExactReachability (transition i₀ j₁) (ctrl i₀): every unitary is a finite word — a one-line composition, not a landed identifier.
ok    4.10 det(permMat swap) = -1 [written: det A(1) = det(A(1/2))² > 0 for any real one-parameter group A, D2 only]
      region swap, |V| = 2, 1 site(s): transpositions = 1, parity = odd
      region swap, |V| = 2, 2 site(s): transpositions = 6, parity = even
      region swap, |V| = 2, 3 site(s): transpositions = 28, parity = even
ok    4.11 single-site binary swap layer is odd (no real-linear flow through it); regions of 2, 3 sites are even
NOTE  4.N3 The CT2 interpolation unit g t (SOC:356) uses e^{iπt}; on the real permutation representation the single-site binary swap gate lies on no one-parameter group (4.10–4.11, continuity-free). The branch is a choice: unit' g t = 1 + (e^{iπ(2k+1)t} − 1)(1−g)/2 also reaches g at t = 1 for every integer k (4.12).
ok    4.12 every odd branch e^{iπ(2k+1)t} reaches the gate at t = 1 (k = 0, 1, -1, 2 exact)
NOTE  4.N4 Region level: swapQ (SwapLayer:372) with swapQ_add_time (SwapLayer:494) is the landed strongly continuous one-parameter group of the swap layer, built from the same unit g t; on the two configurations (0,1), (1,0) of one binary site exchanged by the swap gate it acts as 4.05. The read-write family (ReadWriteControl:87, couple : R → Perm S) is a family of permutations: a continuous map from R to a finite discrete group is constant, so it carries no nontrivial continuous flow (D3 + D6 fail), whatever its parameter law.

=== §5  qutrit control: boundary transitivity is a rank-2 property even in the imported kinematics
ok    5.01 diag(1,0,0) and diag(1/2,1/2,0) are singular (boundary) qutrit states with different spectra
NOTE  5.N1 Written: the minimal face of the qutrit body containing diag(1/2,1/2,0) is a 3-dimensional Bloch ball, that of diag(1,0,0) is a point; affine automorphisms preserve minimal-face dimension. So no affine automorphism of the d = 3 quantum body is transitive on its boundary; boundary transitivity can only be asked of the two-level body.

